    SOFTWARE.


3. THE GENERATED ARTEFACTS — no copyright asserted
---------------------------------------------------

Covers everything under generated/: the unedited responses returned by the
assistants named in each PROMPT.md.

I make no claim of copyright in this material and I am not licensing it, because
I do not believe it is mine to license. The position of the United States
Copyright Office is that copyright requires human authorship, and that writing a
prompt is not by itself authorship: it is closer to instructing a commissioned
artist than to creating the work. On that view these files have no copyright
owner. The law is unsettled and differs between jurisdictions, so treat this as
my honest reading rather than a legal opinion.

What I do assert, as the compiler of the collection, is the selection and
arrangement, which is covered by section 1 above.

If you use these artefacts in teaching, research or writing, cite the archived
snapshot rather than this repository, so that the version you used stays fixed.
That is a request grounded in research practice, not a licence condition.


Why this is separated at all
-----------------------------

This is a book about being careful with generated code. Claiming a licence over
artefacts I did not author, in the repository that exists to prove the book's
claims, would be the same failure the book spends four hundred pages describing:
shipping something that looks correct without checking whether it is.
